[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR bgpd eBGP peering configuration (neighbor remote-as) on router2. Mechanism: During BGP session establishment, each peer’s OPEN message includes its own AS. If the received “peer AS” does not match the locally configured neighbor remote-as, FRR sends a NOTIFICATION “OPEN Message Error/Bad Peer AS” and resets the session to Idle. With the eBGP session down, no inter-AS routes are exchanged, so pc1 (195.11.14.0/24) and pc2 (200.1.1.0/24) cannot reach each other despite the L3 link being up.

Localization and evidence:
- router1: “BGP router identifier 195.11.14.1, local AS number 1” and neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2 (show ip bgp summary). 
- router2 running-config shows “neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 65001” while its local AS is 2. 
- router2 “show ip bgp neighbors 193.10.11.1” reports “BGP state = Idle … Notification sent (OPEN Message Error/Bad Peer AS).”
- router1 “show ip bgp neighbors 193.10.11.2” reports “Notification received (OPEN Message Error/Bad Peer AS).”
- Interfaces 193.10.11.1/24 and 193.10.11.2/24 are up, confirming the transport link is fine.

Root cause: Router2’s neighbor remote-as was misconfigured to 65001 instead of 1 during the change window.

Corrective fix: On router2, set the neighbor to the correct peer AS:
router bgp 2
 no neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 65001
 neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1
Then verify the session reaches Established and that each side receives one prefix (200.1.1.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24). 
=== END ===